PLACES=[
N('christ-church-duanesburg','Christ Episcopal Church, Duanesburg','Duanesburg','sch','worship','standing',42.76861,-74.15472,'exact','open','drive','','1793',
  'A two-story meeting-house church built about 1793 on the Great Western Turnpike (US 20); the square tower with its octagonal spire was added in 1811. General William North (1755–1836), of the North Mansion nearby, is buried in the crypt beneath it. It was listed on the National Register in 1987, and a 2016 visitor during the Landmarks Conservancy\'s Sacred Sites weekend found the old enclosed rented pews still in place.',
  'From outside: the church, its free-standing tower, a carriage shed and the churchyard. Inside, according to a 2016 visitor\'s write-up with photographs, the enclosed (box) pews from the days of pew rental remain, a gallery runs the length of the room, and there is a raised pulpit; the crypt under the church was sealed off long ago. Wikipedia adds a historic pipe organ by Augustus Backus of Troy. The Duanesburg Reformed Presbyterian Church (society 1795, present building 1836 according to its 1935 state marker) stands about 500 feet away on Duanesburg Churches Road. Red brick: not checked.',
  'One of the oldest church interiors in the Capital Region that still has its box pews.',
  'The church belongs to its active Episcopal parish (Christ Church Duanesburg). Its own site lists the Sunday service, which is also broadcast on YouTube, and a self-guided virtual tour; visitors can see the inside at a service. Parking at the church on US 20 at Duanesburg Churches Road (County Route 127) was not checked on a map; the corner is a busy highway junction.',
  [W('Christ Episcopal Church (Duanesburg, New York)'),L('Christ Church Duanesburg (services)','https://www.christchurchduanesburg.org/'),L('HMdb: Christ Episcopal Church','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=5580'),L('Walkabout with Wheels: Sacred Sites visit, 2016 (interior)','https://walkaboutwithwheels.blogspot.com/2016/06/sacred-sites-christ-church-episcopal-in.html'),L('HMdb: Duanesburg Reformed Presbyterian Church','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=41320')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='Holy Eucharist on Sundays at 10 a.m.; weekday opening not checked'),
N('quaker-street-meeting-house','Friends Meeting House, Quaker Street','Duanesburg','sch','worship','standing',42.73467,-74.18658,'site','view','drive','','1807–1813',
  'The Quakers of Duane\'s Bush bought two acres from James C. Duane, son of James Duane, and began this frame meeting house in 1807 to replace a log one; they probably met in it from 1809 and finished it in 1813. Men and women sat on opposite sides of a folding partition raised and lowered by ropes and pulleys, which was taken out in 1885. It stands in the Quaker Street Historic District (1984), whose 61 buildings of about 1807 to 1910 include the McDonald Shoe Factory and Darius Gaige Store.',
  'A plain two-story white building with an attic and a slate roof on a fieldstone foundation with no cellar, beside NY 7, with a carriage shed and burial ground on the property and a 1932 state marker out front. The HMdb page shows the interior during a 2008 bus tour led by the town historian; whether it is open at other times was not checked. Interior not checked beyond those photographs.',
  '',
  'A meeting house of the Religious Society of Friends; see it from NY 7 in the hamlet of Quaker Street. Whether meetings are still held and when visitors may go in was not checked. No parking is mapped beside it; where to stop was not checked.',
  [L('HMdb: Friends Meeting House','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=6043'),W('Quaker Street Historic District')],
  brick='no'),
N('christman-sanctuary','Christman Sanctuary: Will Christman\'s farm on the Bozen Kill','Duanesburg','sch','landmark','traces',42.74083,-74.125,'area','public','hike','','1868–1937',
  'Will Christman (1865–1937), farmer, nature writer and poet, began feeding birds here with his wife Catherine during the blizzard of 1888 and turned the farm into a sanctuary. The Mohawk Valley Hiking Club put up a memorial to the couple in 1931, and The Nature Conservancy bought the first 97 acres from his son and daughter-in-law in 1970. The same year the farm was listed on the National Register as a historic district.',
  'The Register listing names an 1868 frame farmhouse, a stone dairy house, barns, large stone walls and an open lean-to built by the Mohawk Valley Hiking Club. The Nature Conservancy\'s page points visitors to the lean-to and the falls on a yellow spur trail and to the memorial stone on a second yellow side trail; the blue loop (0.7 mile, steep near the falls) crosses red-pine plantation and hemlocks along the Bozen Kill. Which of the farm buildings stand on preserve land and what is left of them was not checked: the pin is the Register\'s point for the district, not a building.',
  '',
  'The Nature Conservancy owns the preserve; open year-round from dawn until dusk. Nine parking spaces at the trailhead on Schoharie Turnpike (County Route 74), about 0.3 mile past the railroad crossing; if full, TNC asks you to use the turnaround at Weaver Street (Route 125) and Schoharie Turnpike. No facilities. The Long Path passes through. The house by the railroad crossing, where the 1931 memorial plaque stands, is a private driveway.',
  [L('The Nature Conservancy: Christman Sanctuary','https://nature.org/en-us/get-involved/how-to-help/places-we-protect/eny-christman-sanctuary'),W('Christman Bird and Wildlife Sanctuary'),L('HMdb: Christman Sanctuary (TNC sign)','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=13904'),L('HMdb: Christman Sanctuary (1931 memorial)','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=7152')],
  th=[42.74313,-74.12866],thn='Christman Sanctuary trailhead, Schoharie Turnpike',brick='?'),
N('herrick-farm-duanesburg','Herrick farm cellar hole, Duanesburg Town Forest','Duanesburg','sch','town','ruins',42.81078,-74.153,'area','public','hike','','1791–1879',
  'In 1791 Stephen Herrick leased 90 acres of forest from James Duane, who then owned most of the town in farm lots; the rent was 13 Spanish milled dollars a year and an apple tree planted for every acre. By 1801 the family had a house and barn here. New York State took the land by mortgage foreclosure in 1879 and sold it to the town in 1934 for one dollar, for reforestation.',
  'A cellar hole and the stone walls of the old fields, with a 2022 Duanesburg Historical Society sign beside the trail. The pin is the sign\'s position as HMdb gives it; the cellar hole itself was not found on the aerial or the LiDAR. The Wiggins farm foundation and family cemetery in the County Forest are on the same trail system, about 1 km south.',
  '',
  'Town of Duanesburg Forest (95 acres), reached by the connecting trail from the Schenectady County Forest Preserve; the county gives over 2.75 miles of trails across the two. The county forest is open dawn to dusk, with limited hunting from November 15 to December 15; the town forest\'s own rules were not checked. Park at the County Forest lot on Lake Road (County Route 88).',
  [L('HMdb: Site of Herrick Farmhouse','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=211294'),CNTY,L('HMdb: Great Lot 92 (County Forest trailhead)','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=42061')],
  th=[42.80174,-74.15425],thn='Schenectady County Forest lot, Lake Road',brick='?'),
N('mariaville-hamlet','Mariaville: Greek Revival hamlet and the Frost house','Duanesburg','sch','town','standing',42.82842,-74.13511,'area','view','drive','','1830s–1850s',
  'Seven buildings of the 1830s to 1850s on the north-west shore of Mariaville Lake, near the Chuctanunda Creek, make up the Mariaville Historic District (1984): five houses, the First Presbyterian Church of Duanesburg and the Silas Marsh General Store, all Greek Revival. A 1935 state marker at the crossroads says James Frost built his house here in 1834 from parts taken out of the Featherstonhaugh mansion of 1808.',
  'A lakeside crossroads of Greek Revival houses, a church and an old store. The Frost marker stands at Mariaville Road (County Route 159) and Batter Street (County Route 94), across from the Mariaville Lakeside Country Store; which building is the Frost house and which the Silas Marsh store was not checked. Interiors not checked.',
  '',
  'A living hamlet of private houses; look from the public roads. No public parking was found on the map, and none was checked on the ground.',
  [W('Mariaville Historic District'),L('HMdb: Frost Homestead','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=6040')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('niskayuna-station','Niskayuna railroad station, Lions Park','Niskayuna','sch','rail','standing',42.77753,-73.82337,'exact','public','walk','stations','1843',
  'The Troy and Schenectady Railroad opened this station in 1843; the line was merged into the New York Central in 1853. It was remodelled in the early 1880s, when the big gabled roof and deep eaves were added. Passenger trains stopped in 1933 and freight in 1984 (Wikipedia gives 1964 as the end of railroad use); in between it was let as a house. It was restored in 1976, listed on the Register in 2007 and now belongs to the Town of Niskayuna beside the Mohawk-Hudson trail.',
  'A one-story red-brick station on a rough quarry-stone foundation, its back wall set right on the steep bank of the Mohawk, with broad eaves over the old platform. A 2025 county marker stands where the trail enters Lions Park. Artists use the station in the warmer months, according to the marker; whether it is open to visitors was not checked.',
  'The 1843 station is older than the New York Central itself.',
  'Town of Niskayuna, on the Mohawk-Hudson Bike-Hike Trail (Empire State Trail) at Lions Park, 3439 Rosendale Road. A small trail lot is mapped right beside it. Park hours not checked.',
  [W('Niskayuna station'),L('HMdb: Niskayuna Rail Station at Lions Park','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=270691')],
  conf='mixed',th=[42.77723,-73.82375],thn='Mohawk-Hudson trail lot, Lions Park',brick='yes'),
N('niskayuna-reformed-church','Niskayuna Reformed Church','Niskayuna','sch','worship','standing',42.77613,-73.83143,'exact','view','drive','cemeteries','1852',
  'A Dutch Reformed congregation founded by Dutch settlers in 1750 built this brick Greek Revival church in 1852. It has brick pilasters and a brick frieze all round and a two-stage tower, square below and an open six-sided belfry above. The churchyard beside it holds burials from the late 1700s.',
  'The church and its old cemetery on NY 7 (3041 Troy-Schenectady Road), with a 2026 marker. Interior not checked. Red brick: the sources say brick but not its colour.',
  '',
  'An active church; see it from Troy-Schenectady Road. Service times and whether visitors may go in or walk the cemetery were not checked. A lot on Niskayuna Road is mapped about 120 m east; whose lot it is was not checked.',
  [W('Niskayuna Reformed Church'),L('HMdb: Reformed Church','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=304249')],
  brick='?'),
N('lock-e7-niskayuna','Barge Canal Lock E7 and the Vischer Ferry Dam','Niskayuna','sch','canal','standing',42.80312,-73.849,'site','public','walk','canal','1907–1914',
  'Lock 7 of the Barge Canal was built from 1907 to 1914 at the south end of a fixed dam that raised the Mohawk about 26 feet to make a channel for barges. The dam is 36 feet high, nearly 2,000 feet long and Z-shaped, running from the power plant to Goat Island and then to the lock. The lock lifts boats 28 feet; until the Vischer Ferry power plant opened across the river in 1925 it made its own electricity with two vertical turbine generators.',
  'The lock chamber, its operating machinery and the long dam across the river, with the Vischer Ferry power plant on the far bank. A 2024 county marker on the bike trail by the lot shows the north lock wall being cast in 1908. Red brick: none mentioned; the lock walls are concrete.',
  '',
  'Lock 7 Park is run by the NYS Canal Corporation (the Town of Niskayuna sends questions to its Albany Division office, 518-449-6036). Park on Lock 7 Road by the bike-path crossing. The canal\'s 2026 season ran May 15 to October 14, with locks working from 8 a.m.; the park\'s own hours were not checked. Keep off the lock walls and gates.',
  [L('HMdb: Lock 7 (Schenectady County marker)','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=266057'),L('HMdb: Vischer Ferry Hydroelectric Power Plant (Lock 7 and the dam)','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=128350'),L('Town of Niskayuna: parks','https://niskayuna.org/departments/parks/'),CANAL],
  th=[42.80312,-73.849],thn='Lock 7 Road lot',brick='no'),
N('lock-e8-rotterdam','Barge Canal Lock E8 and its movable dam','Rotterdam','sch','canal','standing',42.82872,-73.99132,'area','public','walk','canal','1908',
  'Work on Lock 8 began in 1908. Like Locks 8 to 15 on the Mohawk, it was paired with a movable dam on a design taken from the Moldau: a three-span steel truss about 510 feet long from which giant steel plates in frames are swung down by chains onto a concrete sill on the river bed, raising the river as much as 15 feet. In winter the frames are lifted clear and the river runs free.',
  'The lock with its two-story concrete cabins at the corners, which keep the motors and switchgear dry, and the truss of the movable dam, which looks like a bridge but carries only a maintenance walkway. Two county markers stand on the bike path by the lot. The pin is the lot; the lock is beside it.',
  '',
  'NYS Canal Corporation land on the Mohawk-Hudson Bike-Hike Trail (Empire State Trail). Parking at the end of Rice Road. The canal\'s 2026 season ran May 15 to October 14; the dam is up only then. Hours of the lock grounds not checked.',
  [L('HMdb: Erie Canal Lock 8','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=272169'),L('HMdb: The Movable Dam at Lock 8','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=130555'),CANAL],
  th=[42.82869,-73.99154],thn='Lock 8 lot, end of Rice Road',brick='no'),
N('abraham-glen-house','Abraham Glen House (Scotia library)','Scotia','sch','landmark','standing',42.82434,-73.95951,'exact','open','walk','','1730s',
  'A Dutch Colonial heavy-timber-frame house built in the 1730s, rare in the region. It was much altered at the start of the 20th century but keeps its form and original materials, and is now the Scotia branch of the Schenectady County Public Library. It was listed on the National Register in 2004.',
  'A white two-and-a-half-story frame house with a steep gable roof on a mortared fieldstone basement, set diagonally to the road among old trees in Collins Park, with two later wings to the north. The frame is mortise-and-tenon. Inside it is a public library; what old work shows in the rooms was not checked. Red brick: not checked.',
  '',
  'Schenectady County Public Library, Scotia branch, on Mohawk Avenue (NY 5) just west of the Western Gateway Bridge. The county\'s 2024 hours sheet gives Monday, Wednesday, Friday and Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. and Tuesday and Thursday noon to 7 p.m.; after a burst pipe in January 2026 the branch reopened on February 17, 2026 with limited services, so check current hours. Wikipedia places the parking lot north of the house, off Collins Street.',
  [W('Abraham Glen House'),L('SCPL: Scotia branch reopens with limited services (2026)','https://scpl.org/scotia-branch-library-welcomes-patrons-back-with-limited-services/'),L('SCPL hours, 2024 (PDF)','https://schenectadycountyny.gov/sites/default/files/2024-08/2024%20SCPL%20HOURS.pdf')],
  brick='?'),
N('hotel-van-curler-elston-hall','Hotel Van Curler, now Elston Hall','Schenectady','sch','hotel','standing',42.81444,-73.94972,'site','view','walk','hotels','1925',
  'A six-story Classical Revival hotel of 1925, built with money partly from General Electric and the American Locomotive Company, which needed rooms for new workers and wanted a hotel to match those of the big cities. It is now one of the main buildings of Schenectady County Community College, at State Street (NY 5) and Washington Avenue by the Western Gateway Bridge.',
  'An H-shaped brick building trimmed in limestone over a concrete frame, with Palladian windows on the ground floor and a pediment over the five middle bays. Wikipedia says the first floor keeps much of the hotel: the Georgian lobby with columns and panelling, the semicircular Lobby Lounge (now the Mohawk Room) with its original fireplaces and door lunettes, the reception room (now the college president\'s office), the ballroom made from the old dining and assembly rooms, and the one original chandelier left, in a corridor. The upper floors were gutted for the college. Red brick: the colour of the brick is not given.',
  '',
  'Schenectady County Community College. See it from State Street and Washington Avenue; whether visitors may walk into the lobby, and when, was not checked. The Schenectady Armory stands almost opposite and the Stockade begins across the street to the north.',
  [W('Elston Hall')],
  brick='?'),
N('ge-realty-plot','General Electric Realty Plot','Schenectady','sch','town','standing',42.8161,-73.91954,'area','view','walk','','1899–1927',
  'Union College sold this tract to General Electric at the end of the 19th century to pay off a debt. A GE company laid out streets on a plan inspired by Central Park, graded them and laid water and sewers by 1903, and sold lots under covenants setting a minimum lot size and house value. By 1927 about a hundred houses stood here, homes of GE executives and of scientists from the GE research laboratory and Union College, who between them held over 400 patents; two were among the first all-electric houses in the country, used by GE as models. It became a historic district in 1980.',
  'Early-20th-century houses on large set-back lots between Lenox Road, Nott Street, Lowell Road and Union Avenue; only five buildings have been added since the first quarter of the century. Irving Langmuir\'s house is a National Historic Landmark. The Steinmetz Memorial on Wendell Avenue, on the site of Charles Steinmetz\'s house (1297 Wendell Avenue), is the only open space. Which two houses were the all-electric models was not checked.',
  '',
  'A residential neighbourhood of private houses; walk or drive the public streets and look from the sidewalk. Street parking rules not checked.',
  [W('General Electric Realty Plot')],
  brick='?'),
]
